{
  "Breakaway — Product Requirements Document (v3.0)": [
    "Breakaway is an intelligent data aggregator and plan adjuster.",
    "Breakaway maintains a curated library of race-specific plan templates (e.g., Berkeley Hills Road Race, flat criterium, gravel 100-miler). The AI adjusts workouts within these templates based on wearable inputs."
  ],
  "Version History": [
    "Version 1.0 was the Initial PRD, including Whoop + Strava + TrainerRoad + Garmin + MyFitnessPal scope.",
    "Version 1.1 updated the API feasibility, making Strava the primary pipe, native nutrition logger, and TrainerRoad recommendation approach."
  ],
  "v3.0 Change Summary": [
    "Breakaway is positioned as a data aggregator and plan adjuster, not a from-scratch AI plan generator.",
    "Breakaway maintains a curated library of race-specific plan templates (e.g., Berkeley Hills Road Race, flat criterium, gravel 100-miler). The AI adjusts workouts within these templates based on wearable inputs."
  ],
  "1. Executive Summary": [
    "Breakaway aggregates wearable data, maps it to a race-specific coaching template, and tells you exactly what to change — today.",
    "Breakaway assigns a proven, race-specific coaching template to the athlete after they select their target race through a structured onboarding questionnaire.",
    "Every day, Breakaway pulls data from connected wearables, evaluates it against the template, and recommends specific adjustments in plain English through Max — its AI coaching layer."
    ],
  "2.1 The Fragmentation Problem": [
    "TrainerRoad provides structured workouts, FTP progression, and plan templates.",
    "Whoop provides HRV, sleep staging, and recovery score but does not know what the plan says or if a workout is appropriate for the day."
    ],
  "2.2 The Missing Layer": [
    "The missing layer sits above the data and translates it into a single, actionable daily recommendation tied to a specific race goal."
  ],
  "2.3 Why Race-Specific Plans Matter": [
    "Berkeley Hills Road Race is three laps of sustained threshold climbing followed by a VO₂ Max sprint to the line.",
    "A plan built for a flat criterium will produce an athlete who is fast but cannot sustain power for 20 minutes on a climb."
  ]
}

{
  "8.1 Pre-Race Plan (Generated 5–7 Days Before Race)": [
    "Max generates a race plan document the athlete can print or screenshot and take to the start line.",
    "The race plan document is populated from the race_context summary object and the athlete’s current fitness data."
  ],
  "8.2 Fuelling Plan": [
    "Max generates a personalised fuelling schedule based on race duration estimate, individual carb metabolism patterns from the nutrition log history, and standard endurance fuelling guidelines.",
    "The fuelling plan is expressed in concrete, practical terms."
  ],
  "8.3 Race Week Protocol (Activates 7 Days Before Race)": [
    "Race -7 (Mon) focus is a final quality session with a short threshold effort to maintain sharpness.",
    "Race -6 (Tue) focus is active recovery, which is a Zone 1 spin for 30–45 minutes with no efforts."
  ],
  "8.4 Post-Race Analysis": [
    "Max detects the Strava activity after the race and generates a brief analysis.",
    "The analysis includes power file vs. pre-race plan comparison and identified limiters from the data."
  ],
  "9.1 Signal Hierarchy": [
    "Whoop is the primary source for HRV / Recovery data.",
    "Garmin measures HRV opportunistically and is a secondary source for HRV / Recovery data."
  ],
  "9.2 Integration Stack": [
    "Whoop uses the Whoop Developer API v2 (OAuth 2.0) for integration in Phase 1.",
    "Strava uses the Strava API (OAuth 2.0) to aggregate all activities and TSS in Phase 1."
  ],
  "9.3 Why Strava Is the Right Primary Pipe (Phase 1)": [
    "Strava acts as a universal aggregator for training data.",
    "Garmin auto-syncs every activity to Strava, capturing all Garmin data without Garmin approval."
  ]
}

{
  "Phase 3 — Moat Widening  (Months 4–12)": [
    "Google Calendar integration supports life-aware replanning, travel detection, and stress event classification.",
    "Lingo CGM offers real-time glucose monitoring and personalized fuelling timing, pending API access approval."
  ],
  "12.1 API Risks": [
    "Whoop API status is Open, free, and has an active changelog.",
    "Strava API status is Open and well-documented, but policy has tightened for some data types in late 2024."
  ],
  "12.2 Product Risks": [
    "Coaching templates must be reviewed by a domain expert before beta launch.",
    "The initial mitigation for template staleness is an FTP re-test prompt at weeks 4 and 8."
  ],
  "12.3 Key Decisions Still Open": [
    "Pricing options include free personal tool, freemium, or a $12–15/month subscription.",
    "The mobile-first versus web-first decision is open for the dashboard design."
  ],
  "Appendix A: Glossary": [
    "TSS is a measure of workout load combining intensity and duration.",
    "FTP is the Functional Threshold Power, which is the max power a cyclist can sustain for approximately 60 minutes."
  ],
  "Appendix B: Workout Database (Starter Set)": [
    "The Taku workout is in Zone 1 and lasts 30 min for active recovery.",
    "The Pettit workout is in Zone 2 and lasts 60 min for aerobic endurance."
  ]
}

{
  "Appendix C: Sample Prompt — Daily Coaching Query": [
    "The operational format is a complete daily coaching prompt sent to Claude.",
    "The athlete profile has an FTP of 250W and a race of Berkeley Hills Road Race on 2026-04-26."
  ]
}
